<Layout pageTitle="Cell Growth Lab — Model & Methods" description="Mechanochemical neuron-growth equations, axon and dendrite behavior, collision interpretation, source evidence and empirical calibration limits for the Cell Growth Lab." compactHeader>
  <article class="cg-methods">
    <header class="cg-methods-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Cell Growth Lab / {MODEL_VERSION}</p>
      <h1>How a neuron grows</h1>
      <p class="lead">The model, the evidence, and the experiments needed to connect them.</p>
      <nav aria-label="Methods sections"><a href="/simulations/cell-growth/">← Open the laboratory</a><a href="#mechanics">Mechanics</a><a href="#arbors">Axons &amp; dendrites</a><a href="#contexts">Four environments</a><a href="#verification">Verification</a><a href="#references">Primary sources</a></nav>
    </header>

    <section class="callout" aria-label="Model interpretation">
      <h2>What this laboratory represents</h2>
      <p>This is an exploratory, coarse-grained mechanochemical model of neuronal morphology. A neurite grows through local decisions at a growth cone, constrained by its material supply, cytoskeletal state, surroundings and contact history. The visible geometry is the evolving simulated state.</p>
      <p><strong>Biological realism is a calibration question.</strong> Literature supports the mechanisms below; this release has not been fitted to a particular human stem-cell line, organoid preparation, injured nerve or implant. Numerical verification and visually convincing morphology answer different questions.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="evidence">
      <h2>01 / Evidence and model assumptions</h2>
      <p>Mechanisms, parameter choices and validation claims are tracked separately. A measured response in one species can motivate a rule without supplying a transferable numerical constant. This matters especially when comparing a two-dimensional adhesive plate with a three-dimensional tissue-like environment.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Evidence class</th><th>Example</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>{evidence.map(row=><tr>{row.map(value=><td>{value}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <p>For example, the <a href="#clutch">motor-clutch study</a> constrains an Aplysia preparation, while the <a href="#three-d">3D CNS study</a> demonstrates extension without adhesive traction in its matrices. The laboratory uses a reduced model to explore mechanisms; it does not combine these results into a universal law of neuronal growth.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="mechanics">
      <h2>02 / Membrane and cytoskeletal mechanics</h2>
      <p>The membrane has a mechanical role: it surrounds a changing geometry and imposes a cost when growth demands additional surface. Actin-rich protrusions explore the local environment; microtubule support distinguishes an exploratory protrusion from maintained neurite extension. Adhesion transmits force between the cytoskeleton and the environment. <a href="#membrane">Craig et al.</a> and <a href="#clutch">Pulido Cifuentes et al.</a> motivate these coupled processes.</p>
      <h3>Geometry and mechanical degrees of freedom</h3>
      <p>Each soma has 162 moving membrane vertices and 320 triangular faces, with a nominal radius of 6.8–7.7 µm. Actin, adhesion and MT support vary across that surface. A separately moving spherical nucleus is tethered to the cortex and constrained inside it. Neurite roots remain attached to membrane vertices as the cell moves; distal shafts stay anchored. The established shaft network is not a fully deformable beam model, and cell division is not included.</p>
      <details class="equation-panel" open><summary>Growth-cone force balance</summary>
      <p>Let a and u be the actin and microtubule controls, S the cell's dimensionless supply, R its membrane reserve, m the tip's microtubule support, and γ the effective membrane tension. Positive net force permits extension:</p>
      <pre>v_poly = 64 a (0.65 + 0.35 S)                         [µm/h]
F_actin = η max(0, v_poly − v_retro)                    [pN]
F_MT = η v_MT min(a, 1) m                              [pN]
F_membrane = 2π r_eff γ                               [pN]
F_net = F_actin + F_MT − F_membrane                    [pN]
v_tip = max(0, F_net / η) g_kind g_age m_support g_material

η = 3 pN·h/µm; v_MT = 12 µm/h in organoid mode, 8 otherwise
r_eff = 0.52 µm for axons; 0.85 µm for dendrites
g_kind = 1 for axons; 0.58 for dendrites
g_age = clamp((developmental age − 8 h) / 6 h, 0.2, 1)
m_support = 0 when u = 0; otherwise m</pre>
      <p>The material multiplier is min(S, R). Each attempted advance is additionally bounded by the remaining membrane-area budget divided by the current shaft's circumference. A depleted reserve cannot continue feeding extension simply because the tension control is zero.</p>
      <p>The tip-support target is clamp(u(0.35 + 0.65S), 0, 1). Support relaxes exponentially toward it with a 1.8 h time constant, or 1 h when u is zero. Established shaft support relaxes toward min(1, uS) over 8 h. These equations are this lab's reduced closure. They are not a reimplementation of a fitted human growth-cone model. In particular, the zero-actin control also removes the modeled microtubule driving term, which is a model choice and not a universal statement about 3D axon growth.</p>
      <p>The traction readout affects retrograde flow through the closure below. It is not added to the net force a second time. The effective radius in the tension force is a declared parameter; it is not a measured filopodial radius. Directional guidance is a separate steering rule.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="equation-panel"><summary>Quasi-steady motor-clutch closure</summary>
      <pre>k_eff = k_substrate k_clutch / (k_substrate + k_clutch)
f_bond = F_b log(1 + k_eff (v_retro/3600) / (k_off,0 F_b))
k_off = k_off,0 exp(min(15, f_bond/F_b))
occupancy = k_on / (k_on + k_off)
F_traction = N_clutch adhesion occupancy f_bond
v_target = v_unloaded / (1 + F_traction/F_stall)
v_retro ← 0.5 v_retro + 0.5 v_target    (24 iterations)</pre>
      <p>The iteration starts at v_unloaded. Speeds are converted from µm/h to µm/s for the bond calculation. The baseline values are {baseline.unloadedRetrogradeUmPerHour} µm/h, {baseline.clutchSpringPnPerUm} pN/µm for clutch stiffness, {baseline.clutchOnPerSecond} s⁻¹ for binding, {baseline.clutchOffPerSecond} s⁻¹ for unloaded unbinding, {baseline.bondForcePn} pN for the bond force scale, {baseline.motorStallPn} pN for the effective stall scale, and {baseline.clutchSites} available clutch sites.</p>
      <p>This is a deterministic mean-field approximation inspired by the mechanism in <a href="#clutch">the source literature</a>. It does not track individual bonds or reproduce a published stochastic motor-clutch simulation. The model's substrate setting is an <strong>effective spring stiffness in pN/µm</strong>; it is not a Young's modulus in kPa.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="equation-panel"><summary>Soma shell and material supply</summary>
      <pre>E = γ A + KA/(2 A0) (A − A0)² + KV/(2 V0) (V − V0)²
    − p0 (V − V0) + Σ_edges ke/2 (l − l0)² + kb/2 Σ_i |Lx_i|²
F_passive = −∇E
x_i ← x_i + Δt F_i / ζ
p0 = 2γ / cbrt(3V0 / (4π))</pre>
      <p>KA = 45 pN/µm; KV = 180 pN/µm²; ke = 48 × cortex elasticity pN/µm; kb = 24 × curvature resistance pN/µm; ζ = 3 pN·h/µm. L is the fixed umbrella graph Laplacian, and the implementation uses its full transpose in the force calculation. This is a discrete curvature regularizer, not a mesh-converged Helfrich bending modulus. Area and volume targets come from the initial mesh. The previous radial clamp has been removed.</p>
      <p>The overdamped solver uses substeps at most 0.0125 h. A line search guards face orientation, triangle quality, volume collapse, contact constraints, material availability and root attachments. Strain and mesh quality appear in the inspector; JSON retains rejected steps. These guards do not establish stability for every possible parameter combination.</p>
      <p>The nucleus has radius 0.38 times nominal cell radius, drag 45 pN·h/µm and tether stiffness 70 pN/µm. Coarse MT support is represented by radial springs. Equal and opposite internal forces act on cortex and nucleus. The nucleus is a rigid sphere; its envelope and chromatin are not separately resolved.</p>
      <p>Each cell's extension budget has a capacity of 1.5 times its initial soma-mesh area. New length consumes the corresponding cylindrical area demand; 60% of withdrawn cylindrical area can return to available capacity during retraction. Initial seed processes are supplied as part of the starting construction. The saved state tracks supplied, used and recycled area. The shell's surface area and the extension reserve remain separate quantities; this budget does not resolve all lipids or radius changes in the whole cell.</p>
      <p>Supply S is a generic capacity variable, not a measured molecule concentration. Outside organoid mode, support(position) is 1. Inside it, support is clamp(distance from center / 100 µm, 0.28, 1). This radial rule approximates reduced central support; it does not solve oxygen diffusion or metabolism. Extension also decreases S by 0.0007 per µm, with S constrained to [0, 1].</p>
      </details>
      <h3>Adhesion, shape change and cell migration</h3>
      <p>Cell polarity defines a leading and trailing side. Local actin state relaxes toward 0.12 + 0.88 front² over 1.2 h, while MT support responds over 3 h. Active protrusion and rear contraction are internally balanced: their mean force is removed. Only transmitted substrate or matrix traction supplies active net force. Traction vanishes when adhesion is zero.</p>
      <pre>access = exp(−gap_to_dish / 2 µm) on the plate; 1 in matrix modes
on = 5 adhesion (0.15 + front) access                  [h⁻¹]
off = 0.8 (1 + 4 rear contractility)                  [h⁻¹]
b_next = on/(on+off) + (b − on/(on+off)) exp(−(on+off) Δt)
T_i = gain Fmotor b_i front² stiffness/(stiffness+100)
      / (1 + 2 adhesion²)                            [pN]</pre>
      <p>Fmotor is 620 pN on the dish and 110 pN for the effective matrix coupling. The gain combines actin, motility and a developmental factor: 1 for stem-like/precursor cells, 0.45 immature, 0.20 polarized and 0.08 maturing. The cortical network distributes 85% of total traction; 15% remains local. Dish adhesion also pulls the basal cortex toward the nonpenetrating substrate plane. These are declared exploratory closures, not measured human-cell parameters.</p>
      <p>Short-range cell adhesion acts within 7 µm of conservative soma envelopes. Contact inhibition rotates polarity, and contact constraints resist movement. Clusters are measured from local contacts; a final cluster position is never specified. The <a href="#motility">deformable-cell literature</a> motivates these mechanisms, but does not validate this particular reduction.</p>
      <h3>Mechanical membrane is distinct from electrical membrane</h3>
      <p>Surface area, tension, deformation and contact belong to the growth model. Membrane voltage, ion-channel currents, action potentials and extracellular recordings require a separate electrical model. This laboratory does not infer any of those signals from morphology or color. Its membrane visualization does not imply that a lipid bilayer, individual channels or molecular actin filaments are resolved.</p>
      <h3>Why force balance is overdamped</h3>
      <p>Growth is represented as directed, resisted movement. Contact changes the admissible direction, local forces or growth state. It is not modeled as a fast rigid-body impact with a coefficient of restitution. A stalled tip can continue probing; a retracting tip removes distal structure instead of creating a backwards extension.</p>
      <p>Substrate stiffness is not a universal growth accelerator. Its effect depends on force transmission, adhesion and the other terms in the model. A parameter sweep should inspect extension, branching, contact and resource use together; a single attractive final arbor cannot identify the causal mechanism.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="development">
      <h2>03 / From precursor to a polarized neuron</h2>
      <p>The precursor starting state describes developmental competence in the model. It does not simulate reprogramming, gene regulation, pluripotency, lineage specification or the laboratory steps of a human differentiation protocol. Developmental stages gate when processes can emerge and acquire axonal or dendritic behavior.</p>
      <p><a href="#polarity">Local microtubule stabilization</a> is one experimentally supported contributor to axon specification. The laboratory reduces this complex process to explicit developmental state rules. A rule that selects an axon is not proof that the model has recovered the entire polarity signaling network.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Starting state</th><th>Precursor begins</th><th>Initial neurites</th><th>Axon assignment</th><th>“Maturing” label</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Stem-like starting state</td><td>48 h</td><td>56 h</td><td>66 h</td><td>96 h</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Neural precursor</td><td>0 h</td><td>8 h</td><td>18 h</td><td>48 h</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Immature neuron</td><td>Earlier assumed stage</td><td>0 h</td><td>10 h</td><td>40 h</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <p>Initiation chooses three to five separated, actin/MT-weighted sites on the actual membrane. This produces variable process origins rather than an identical radial star. At the assigned polarity time, the root arbor with the largest score, total arbor length + 6 µm × alignment with the cue direction, becomes the axon arbor. This is an explicit heuristic, not a molecular symmetry-breaking calculation. Its existing segments change identity together; the others retain dendritic behavior. The “maturing” label records developmental age and does not imply functional electrical maturity.</p>
      <p>The developmental clock is an assumed model clock. <a href="#human">Human iPSC-derived neurons</a> can share early morphological stages with rodent cultures while following different growth kinetics. “24 h” in a displayed experiment therefore means 24 modeled hours under the selected rules, not a forecast that a stem cell becomes a mature human neuron in one day.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="arbors">
      <h2>04 / Axons and dendrites need different rules</h2>
      <p>A neuron's axon and dendrites share cytoskeletal machinery but serve different geometric roles. The lab treats process identity as part of the state, so differences in extension, steering, branching, taper and contact can be examined directly. These are representative behaviors, not a single morphology shared by every neuronal class.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Implemented rule</th><th>Axon</th><th>Dendrite</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Extension gain</td><td>1</td><td>0.58</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Baseline branch hazard</td><td>0.012 h⁻¹</td><td>0.045 h⁻¹</td></tr>
      <tr><td>New branch direction</td><td>0.35–1.00 rad from local direction</td><td>0.35–1.35 rad from local direction</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Root / minimum shaft radius</td><td>0.52 / 0.23 µm</td><td>1.05 / 0.30 µm</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Nearby-process steering</td><td>Adjustable alignment to another cell's axon</td><td>Adjustable avoidance of the cell's own processes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Low-growth retraction rate</td><td>1.5 µm/h</td><td>2.5 µm/h</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
      <pre>λ = λ_kind branchingGain S m / (1 + 0.3 branchOrder)
p_branch = 1 − exp(−λ Δt)</pre>
      <p>Independent local growth episodes alternate advance, pauses and brief retractions. The Growth episodes control blends from continuous drive to asynchronous activity. Exploratory projections and cone rims change shape locally. Plate-grown tips can follow a raised route over an established shaft, subject to finite-radius collision checks; distal shafts remain anchored. These video-informed rules are not fitted rates or a biochemical actin-wave solver.</p>
      <p>Branching requires an extending tip at least 3 h old, microtubule support ≥0.45, reserve ≥0.2, nonzero actin and microtubule controls, and available tip capacity. Events can bifurcate the leading tip or create a lateral bud at an eligible retained shaft node more than 7 µm behind it. Lateral events preserve the leading process; branch angles vary. These rules are qualitative approximations, not measured event frequencies. Branch order is limited to five, and the default active-tip cap is {DEFAULT_CONFIG.maxTipsPerCell} per cell. Those limits are computational/model choices.</p>
      <p>Steering combines the current direction with cue, nearby-process, obstacle and environment terms. Same-cell dendrite avoidance and other-cell axon alignment operate within 12 µm. Directional fluctuations use an Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process with a 1.5 h correlation time. These are dimensionless steering weights, not solved receptor kinetics or a three-dimensional tissue-stress field.</p>
      <p>When calculated speed is below 0.02 µm/h for over 2 h, specified low-driver, low-reserve or negative-force conditions trigger retraction. Persistent non-neurite collision after 1.8 h triggers 2.2 µm/h retraction. The distal endpoint retreats along its existing path; a parent with surviving children is retained. The model does not implement activity-dependent pruning or branch survival determined by functional synapses.</p>
      <h3>Read dendrite growth as a history</h3>
      <p>An arbor's final branch count hides how it was assembled. Record branch births, retractions, surviving tips, cable length and the spatial distribution of branches. The <a href="#dendrites">growth-and-retraction study</a> demonstrates why pruning is part of development rather than merely a correction to an already completed tree.</p>
      <p>Self-avoidance is cell-identity dependent. Branches from the same neuron can respond differently from processes belonging to a neighbor. <a href="#self-avoidance">Mammalian protocadherin experiments</a> support this distinction in specific cell classes. A global repulsion rule applied equally to every dendrite would erase it.</p>
      <h3>Read axon growth as extension, guidance and branching</h3>
      <p>Measure path length alongside displacement and tortuosity. An axon can accumulate cable while making little progress toward a target. Branch initiation must also be distinguished from extension: <a href="#branching">cytoskeletal perturbations in cortical neurons</a> can affect these processes differently.</p>
      <p>An axon that follows an existing process has a different encounter history from one that turns away from an obstacle. Alignment is a geometric model behavior; it is not a molecular assay of a particular cell-adhesion molecule.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="contacts">
      <h2>05 / Collision, recognition and connection</h2>
      <p>Three questions must remain separate: do two finite structures physically intersect; how does a tip respond to that encounter; and does a biological connection eventually function? The first is geometry, the second is a modeled behavioral response, and the third requires evidence beyond geometry.</p>
      <p>A spatial hash finds candidate segments over their full bounding boxes. Proposed extensions are checked along the complete swept path: sphere intersections for round obstacles and conservative soma envelopes, finite-capsule intersections for neurites, and slab intersections for expanded boxes. Expanded box corners are conservative; the soma exclusion radius is the maximum of its current vertex distances and 1.06 times its nominal radius. This conservative envelope can overexclude narrow passages. Nearby parent segments and shared branch junctions have explicit exclusions. A separate check on the proposed terminal-shaft chord prevents an endpoint update from cutting through a corner.</p>
      <p>Rigid obstacles, somata and world boundaries exclude the effective <strong>growth-cone radius</strong>: the maximum radius of the twelve-node, force-dependent growth-cone rim (initially 1.8 µm for axons and 2.1 µm for dendrites). Established neurites use finite shaft radii. A moving cone additionally excludes foreign shafts and other cells’ active cones at its current rim radius; local contact can compress the rim. Same-cell branch junctions share material. The rim relaxes under effective actin, MT and membrane loads. It is a coarse radial model, not a molecular lamellipodium. Five finite-radius exploratory protrusions per cone relax their length over 0.4 h toward an actin- and load-dependent target, sweep against solids, foreign shafts, somata and boundaries, and bias subsequent steering away from blocked directions. These are effective geometric sensing degrees of freedom, not individually resolved actin polymers. A collision clips growth before entry, turns direction toward a tangent, and accumulates a stall interval. Established shafts are not subsequently relaxed against each other as deformable rods.</p>
      <p>Opposite-kind processes from different cells can produce an apposition record. A cell-adhesion gain of at least 0.3 holds a contacted heterocellular axon–dendrite tip while it remains apposed; this is a phenomenological stabilization rule. Continuous apposition for {baseline.appositionHours} modeled hours promotes it to a candidate. Interrupted encounter resets the accumulated dwell interval. The current linked segments are checked every solver step: the record becomes lost if either segment disappears or their closest surface separation exceeds 0.5 µm (including the active cone envelope at a terminal contact). A renewed encounter after loss begins a new dwell interval. A candidate therefore requires continuing geometric proximity; no mechanical bond or synaptic machinery is solved. Neither axon–axon contact nor same-cell contact is counted as a candidate synapse.</p>
      <p><a href="#collision">Mechanical-impact experiments</a> support collapse and retraction as possible growth-cone responses. They do not imply that every touch causes collapse or that a force threshold can be transferred between assays without a contact-area model.</p>
      <p><strong>A candidate connection is an anatomical hypothesis.</strong> A stable apposition does not prove synaptic vesicle release, postsynaptic receptors, signal transmission, target specificity or useful network computation. <a href="#connections">Developmental synaptic receptivity</a> depends on maturation and interactions beyond dendrite extension alone. No electrical synapse is silently created by a crossing in this lab.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="contexts">
      <h2>06 / Four environments, four interpretation limits</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Environment</th><th>Representation</th><th>Useful questions</th><th>Outside the model</th></tr></thead><tbody>{contexts.map(row=><tr>{row.map(value=><td>{value}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <p>Environmental presets are experiments with the same evolving cell representation. Agreement with plate data would not validate an organoid, a regeneration gap or a biohybrid construct. Each context needs its own geometry, boundary conditions, measured parameters and held-out comparison.</p>
      <p>Myelination is deferred. The current experiment focuses on unmyelinated neurite development and contact. It does not solve oligodendrocyte or Schwann-cell wrapping, internode selection, g-ratio, saltatory conduction or remyelination.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="measurements">
      <h2>07 / What to measure</h2>
      <p>V2 adds centroid displacement, traveled path, speed, traction, adhesion occupancy, area/volume strain, mesh quality, contact clusters, sister-branch angles and x–z projected dendritic convex-hull area. The engine also records separate axonal and dendritic cable lengths, surviving branch nodes, active tips, branch-order counts, longest paths, radial reach, shaft area, signed cone speed, separate extension/retraction rates, live/allocated segments, appositions and candidates, membrane area, tension and reserve. Longest paths begin at the neurite root on the soma surface; radial reach is measured from the soma center. Sholl intersections use exact segment–sphere crossings at radii of 15, 30, 45, 60, 90, 120, 160, 200 and 260 µm around each soma. The recorded profiles include combined processes, axons alone and dendrites alone; the workbench compares the two identities separately.</p>
      <p>The default WebGL surface renderer and automatic software 3D fallback read the same solved membrane and nucleus geometry. Both provide rotation, close-up inspection and a micrometre scale bar. Four microscopy-inspired modes change color and lighting; no optical image-formation physics is implied. Radius settings of 2× and 3× enlarge only the display, leaving collisions and measurements unchanged. Nuclei have simulated positions and physical exclusion radii. Individual internal filament lines illustrate coarse MT support and do not represent independently solved polymers. A projected crossing does not by itself establish a physical contact.</p>
      <p>Collision episodes combine consecutive hits by a tip on the same modeled object; changing object or a gap longer than 1.5 timesteps begins another episode. The internal collision-step counter and retraction-step counter remain update counts. None is a count of unique biological cell pairs. Tortuosity and full branch lifetimes can be analyzed separately from exported geometry and saved states.</p>
      <p>The JSON preserves coordinates, topology and state; the CSV summarizes recorded history; SWC exports one selected cell's geometry for external analysis. SWC does not encode force states, a lipid surface, or validated electrical parameters. Shaft area is a cylindrical lateral-area sum and is separate from the triangulated soma area.</p>
      <p>For a mechanical experiment, inspect membrane demand and the force terms that caused movement. Compare matched starting conditions, keep the seed, and change one mechanism at a time before testing interactions among controls. Summary history is sampled approximately hourly and retains at most 1,450 entries; the event log retains the latest 300 events. These are bounded summaries, not a full continuous microscopy movie.</p>
      <p>Different seeds represent different realizations of the model's stochastic rules. They are not independent biological donors, and their spread is not a clinical confidence interval. A parameter sweep can reveal sensitivity inside the model without establishing how likely a real culture is to behave that way.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="verification">
      <h2>08 / Verification and the next calibration experiment</h2>
      <p>Numerical verification asks whether finite-radius obstacles are detected between timesteps, whether branch topology remains valid, whether states stay finite, whether resources constrain growth, and whether saving and resuming preserves an experiment. Refinement checks examine whether results change as a numerical step is reduced. These checks support implementation correctness within their tested conditions.</p>
      <p>The generated release report records <strong>{verification.checks.filter(check=>check.passed).length} passing acceptance checks out of {verification.checks.length}</strong> for {verification.modelVersion}. Read the <a href="https://github.com/corbins-cryo-pod/buildthesimulation/blob/main/src/data/cell-growth-validation.json">quantitative report</a> and <a href="https://github.com/corbins-cryo-pod/buildthesimulation/blob/main/docs/cell-growth/VALIDATION.md">declared criteria</a>. Source hashes and the actual runtime are retained in the report.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Analytic geometry case</th><th>Absolute error</th><th>Acceptance</th></tr></thead><tbody>{verification.analyticGeometry.map(row=><tr><td>{row.geometry}</td><td>{row.absoluteErrorUm.toExponential(2)} µm</td><td>≤ {verification.acceptance.analyticGeometryUm} µm</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <h3>Deformable-cell verification</h3>
      <p>The <a href="https://github.com/corbins-cryo-pod/buildthesimulation/blob/main/src/data/cell-growth-mechanics-validation.json">mechanical verification report</a> adds {mechanicsVerification.checks.filter(c=>c.passed).length} passing checks: analytic energy gradients, passive and unbound controls, directional migration, nucleus containment, area/volume constraints, exact continuation, stress tests and actual mechanical-step refinement. At default gains the polarized single-cell control moves {number(mechanicsVerification.measurements[0].displacementUm[0],2)} µm in eight model hours. These are model outcomes, not measured precursor-cell speeds.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Mechanical integration step</th><th>Centroid difference from finest</th><th>Vertex RMS difference from finest</th></tr></thead><tbody>{mechanicsVerification.mechanicalStepResults.map(r=><tr><td>{r.maxStepHours} h</td><td>{number(r.centroidDifferenceUm,5)} µm</td><td>{number(r.vertexRmsDifferenceUm,5)} µm</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <p>This four-hour control varies the internal mechanical step itself. Varying only the outer growth timestep is a separate subcycling-invariance test. Neither demonstrates spatial convergence of the fixed 162-vertex cortex. Extreme high-drive, low-stiffness settings can reach the mesh-quality guard and reject steps; the inspector reports these constraints instead of silently accepting folded geometry.</p>
      <h3>Environment studies and allocation limits</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Environment</th><th>Requested time</th><th>Simulated time</th><th>Segment budget</th><th>Termination</th></tr></thead><tbody>{verification.environments.map(row=><tr><td>{PRESETS.find(preset=>preset.id===row.mode)?.title ?? row.mode}</td><td>{number(row.requestedHours,1)} h</td><td>{number(row.simulatedHours,2)} h</td><td>{row.config.maxSegments.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td><td>{row.limits.reached ? row.limits.reason : 'Requested duration reached'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <p>These are the exact configurations retained in the generated report. An allocation cap is a computational stopping condition. A run that stops at its segment budget has not completed the remaining requested biological time.</p>
      <h3>Deterministic timestep refinement</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Final cable length</th><th>Condition</th></tr></thead><tbody>{verification.timestepRefinement.cases.map(row=><tr><td>{row.dtHours} h</td><td>{number(row.finalLengthUm,4)} µm</td><td>{verification.timestepRefinement.durationHours} h, one immature cell, branching and noise disabled</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <p>The coarse-to-finest length difference is {number(100*verification.timestepRefinement.relativeError,3)}%, against a {100*verification.acceptance.deterministicLengthRelativeError}% limit. The intermediate step is closer to the finest-step result. This is a bounded continuous-growth refinement study, not proof of convergence of every stochastic arbor, contact history or membrane shape. The finest solution is a numerical reference, not an exact analytic answer.</p>
      <p>The same-seed and save/resume checks compare states inside the same JavaScript runtime. Cross-browser bit identity is not promised. Stochastic event sampling and the order of interacting tips can make individual trajectories diverge when the numerical step or population is changed.</p>
      <pre>node --test tests/cell-growth.mjs tests/cell-growth-mechanics.mjs
node scripts/validate-cell-growth.mjs
node scripts/validate-cell-growth-mechanics.mjs
npm run build</pre>
      <p>The default step is {DEFAULT_CONFIG.dtHours} h. A new shaft segment is created after its predecessor reaches approximately {baseline.shaftSegmentUm} µm; an individual advance can exceed that nominal length. Swept collision checks still examine the complete proposed movement. Geometry and branching can change at large steps, so the declared refinement result should not be generalized beyond its conditions. The global allocation budget is {DEFAULT_CONFIG.maxSegments.toLocaleString('en-US')} segments, used by the culture and regeneration presets. The organoid preset uses {organoidSegmentBudget.toLocaleString('en-US')} and the biohybrid preset uses {biohybridSegmentBudget.toLocaleString('en-US')}. These budgets include retired segments; reaching the chosen budget stops further stepping.</p>
      <h3>Experimental scale comparison — not calibration</h3>
      <p>Koch et al. reported mean net traction of 71 ± 15 pN (SE) for their hippocampal growth cones and 284 ± 58 pN for early DRG cultures. Their actin flow measurements on laminin-coated glass were 9.5 ± 0.3 and 3.4 ± 0.3 µm/min, respectively. This lab starts from an exploratory unloaded flow of 1.10 µm/min; its effective spring setting is not their hydrogel modulus. These differences and unlike conditions prevent a quantitative validation claim. Whole-cell motor traction must not be compared with a single growth cone's traction.</p>
      <p>The strongest remaining limitations are uncalibrated kinetics, conservative soma envelopes that can overexclude narrow gaps, a fixed cortex topology, reduced radial growth cones, and substrate-anchored shafts without full bending mechanics. No functional synapses, full organoid development or myelination are claimed.</p>
      <h3>A minimum empirical calibration plan</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Choose a preparation.</strong> Specify species, neuronal class, cell line, differentiation stage, matrix, coating, density and imaging interval. Do not pool unlike preparations into one default.</li>
        <li><strong>Track development over time.</strong> Annotate somata, axons, dendrites, branch births and losses, extension/retraction velocities, turning angles and contact outcomes. Retain failed and stalled trajectories.</li>
        <li><strong>Constrain mechanisms.</strong> Obtain membrane-area or tension measurements where feasible, actin-flow or traction measurements, and microtubule-state measurements. Morphology alone cannot uniquely identify all mechanical coefficients.</li>
        <li><strong>Fit and test separately.</strong> Fit a limited parameter set to one subset of experiments, lock the model, and evaluate held-out cells, times and perturbations. Report residuals and uncertainty rather than only representative images.</li>
        <li><strong>Validate the environment and the connection claim.</strong> Repeat the process for each context. Add paired electrophysiology or appropriately controlled functional imaging before assigning functional synapses to geometric appositions.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>A useful first acceptance target is simultaneous agreement with extension-speed distributions, branch lifetimes, Sholl profiles and encounter outcomes. Matching only total length is underconstrained: multiple wrong combinations of growth and retraction can produce the same length.</p>
    </section>

